it's 2021 how many Ford self-driving  cars are on the road and what are they  doing well we're going to start mass  producing a fully autonomous vehicle  what we call a level 4 vehicle which  means it won't have a steering wheel  won't have a gas pedal or a brake brake  pedal and we see the first applications  kind of commercial applications whether  a ride herring or ride-sharing service  and in in major cities so what 2021 is  is where our intent is to holiday do you  imagine the fleet is at that point well  it'll it will start off fairly modest as  as we start you know signing up  customers and also being able to  populate some of the cities with these  vehicles but we see it growing over time  and and and being a very good business  for us and also providing a lot of  societal benefits for folks so you're  doubling your workforce in Silicon  Valley you're investing in four  different technology companies to help  get you there this suggests more of a  go-it-alone approach when it comes to  self-driving cars why take that route  well actually it's we're asking  ourselves three very important questions  when it comes to the development of  autonomous vehicles and in a lot of  different areas one is where do we want  to play second is how do we want to win  and third is what are the capabilities  that we need so when you look at  autonomous vehicles we've been at this  for over ten years so we have a lot of  engineering know-how but as you can see  from some of the partnering we're doing  with some of these great technical  partners we have said in some cases we  can't do it all ourselves so we're  partnering with a lot of different  companies to allow us to realize that  vision of getting fully autonomous  vehicles on the road that said we've  seen you do a lot of small deals rather  than one or two big ones why is that do  you want to avoid sharing the wheel with  another big company not at all we first  off as we think about how we're going  about this we're not in a race to make  announcements what we are in a race to  do is do what's right for customers and  do rights what we do what's right for  our business and so we're looking at the  technical technology roadmap for  delivering fully autonomous vehicles and  seeking out partners that will help fill  in the spaces for us that we've worked  on for the last ten years so we've seen  so many partnerships so many big  investments in the car industry plus the  tech industry you've got GM buying  cruise automation for a billion dollars  investing five hundred million dollars  in lift Apple investing a billion  dollars in DD Volkswagen and get Daimler  and my taxi why haven't we seen you do  deals like that well again we're not in  a race to make announcements we're when  our race to do what's right for you know  as we want to serve our customers are  you open to deals like we we have always  said that we are open to everything  everything is on the table for us but it  has to make as we think about those  things it has to make sense for our  brand it has to create value for our  company and that's the lens in which we  would look through but we talk with  everybody just like everybody else so  how do you imagine getting into the  ride-sharing business or working with a  company like uber without a deal like  that well in some cases we're doing some  some pilots right now we've done some  ride sharing and car sharing pilots for  the last year and a half some with  vehicles some with the shuttles and  we'll continue to do that and at the  same time talk keep talking with others  and see what what makes sense from a  from a business model standpoint in  terms of how can we make a good business  opportunity out of this but also at the  same time fulfill a societal need Google  remains a big and early player in the  self-driving car space why didn't a deal  between Ford and Google work out and  could a deal still happen well we talked  with everybody and as you can as you can  expect those those conversations are  private but again we always run through  the lens of whoever we talk to does it  make business sense for us does it help  build our brand and also is there a  cultural fit as well what do you make of  the fact that Google and Apple are very  much approaching this as you know  building platforms for the car platforms  for potentially self-driving cars rather  than actually making cars well again I  can't speak for Google or whatever Apple  is doing and clearly that's their  approach from the business they have now  I think there's an underestimation here  I think you know a lot of people think  oh well the tech companies will just  come in and  and you know do better than the car  companies listen we respect all of our  competitors but there's the brains of  the vehicle the virtual driver system  and then there's a vehicle platform we  are the ultimate systems integrator and  we've been doing this for over a hundred  years so if that's their approach what  would make it worthwhile for you to get  involved with one of them your point you  know overall as we said is we look at  the technology look at the roadmaps if  there's some things that others do  better than we do we're open to  partnering and just like we've announced  today for new partners now Tesla is very  much focused on a more semi autonomous  model and you know there's a safety  investigation going on related to one of  their crashes in which someone died  using autopilot you guys are focused on  fully autonomous Google is focused on  fully autonomous your car isn't even  going to have a steering wheel why is  that the best way why not go the semi  autonomous well I can't speak for Tesla  and and clearly we're a leader and what  we call level zero through to us driver  assist features features that will keep  you in your lane those type of things  stop you in traffic stop and go traffic  and we're going to continue to be a  leader there and continue to invest when  in level four we have a lot of  confidence and we believe in our plan  that taking the driver out of the loop  is really important because this little  no-man's land at what point do you have  to reengage the driver in a level-3 type  of vehicle we're kind of struggling with  understanding how do you do that  responsibly and timely for the driver  and that's why we're focused on level  four so someday once you get your cars  on the road afford self-driving car  we'll get involved in an accident it  will be rare but it will happen when  that happens who's liable well as we go  forward we have to have a discussion  with  the regulator Z the consumer advocate  groups insurance companies etc and  clearly this is one of the areas that  we're going to have to all work together  to come up with good policies both  regulatory policies but also legal  policies now one of the investments that  you've announced today you're making  with Baidu and allied our company mmm I  spoke to the head of the self-driving  car unit at I do who said they want cars  on the road by 2021 as well will this  investment with Baidu help you get Ford  self-driving cars on the road in China  well let's put some perspective this we  we co-lead the next series of investment  for for Bella dine we were not partners  with Baidu they're just one of the  financial partners there so I wouldn't  read anything more into that but what  about your China strategy or are trying  to strategy we have a good business in  China it's growing and as we think about  mobility solutions there you know we're  thinking about how do we play a role in  that in addition to our core business of  selling great cars and trucks
 

